      "kind": "lede",
      "html": "<p>A risk survey has one reader who matters. The underwriter. They never see the building. They see your report. So the value of the survey depends on how much of what you saw reaches the page. The gap between the visit and the write-up is the enemy.</p>"
    },
    {
      "kind": "takeaways",
      "heading": "The short version",
      "html": "<ul><li>A risk survey helps the insurer price the risk and set terms.</li><li>COPE (construction, occupancy, protection, exposure) is the backbone of most reports.</li><li>Protections and their upkeep are the details that most affect the rate.</li><li>Recommendations, with a priority and a date, are your added value.</li><li>Record on site. Every hour before write-up loses detail.</li><li>Quickler writes the report from what you send. Your judgement is still the survey. You still sign it.</li></ul>"
    },
    {
      "kind": "default",
      "eyebrow": "The purpose",
      "heading": "What a risk survey is for",
      "html": "<p>A survey lets an underwriter understand a risk they will never visit. It supports the price, the terms, and whether to write the business at all. On bigger or riskier sites a visit is often needed before a quote.</p><p>The picture rests on COPE. Construction: what the building is made of and how it behaves in a fire. Occupancy: the trade inside and its hazards. Protection: fire and security systems. Exposure: what surrounds the site and could spread a loss. Get these four right and the underwriter has what they need.</p>"
    },
    {
      "kind": "default",
      "eyebrow": "The detail that moves the rate",
      "heading": "Protections and their upkeep",
      "html": "<p>The protection section often moves the rate most. A sprinkler system that is designed well and maintained is worth a lot. One that is out of service, or never tested, is worth little. The report must say which. The same goes for intruder alarms, fire detection and security.</p><p>These facts fade fastest. Did the alarm panel show a fault, or was that another site last week? A photo of the panel and the service sticker, taken on the spot, settles it.</p>"
    },
    {
      "kind": "default",
      "eyebrow": "The added value",
      "heading": "Recommendations that get acted on",
      "html": "<p>A good survey also recommends changes. Fit a fire door. Service the sprinklers. Clear stock from the boiler room. This is where you add value, because the underwriter can make them conditions of cover. \"Improve housekeeping\" is easy to ignore. A clear one with a priority and a date gets tracked.</p><p>Quickler asks for each recommendation on its own, with its priority and date. The report lists them clearly. Each becomes a job to fix.</p>"
    },
